Stable Angina
Cardiovascular
Predictable exertional chest pain, Relief with rest/nitrates within 10 minutes, Transient ST depression

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CDV-040
Reason for Visit: Recurrent, predictable heavy chest pain when walking, causing significant anxiety regarding a potential heart attack.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient reporting heavy retrosternal chest pain that radiates to the arm. While the pain is currently resolved, urgent after-hours physical assessment was strictly required to clinically rule out acute coronary syndrome (unstable angina), perform a baseline physical examination, and ensure the patient was hemodynamically stable and safe to remain in the community overnight prior to formal stress testing.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "Every time I walk up the slight hill to my house, I get this heavy squeezing in my chest that goes down my left arm, but it completely disappears if I stop and rest for five minutes."
History of Present Illness: Patient (aged 60) reports a 4-week history of predictable retrosternal squeezing and heavy chest pain. The pain is provoked by physical exertion, specifically demonstrating a consistent, reproducible threshold of activity (walking exactly 200 meters up a slight incline). Discomfort radiates to the ulnar aspect of the left arm. There is complete resolution of pain within 5 to 10 minutes of standing still to rest. Patient notes shortness of breath correlating exactly with the exertion and pain. Exacerbation of symptoms noted during cold weather walks. Crucially, there is a complete absence of pain at rest. KEY SYMPTOMS: Predictable retrosternal squeezing or heavy chest pain provoked by physical exertion. Consistent reproducible threshold of activity required to elicit symptoms. Discomfort radiating to the neck, jaw, or ulnar aspect of the left arm. Complete resolution of pain within 5 to 10 minutes of rest. Absence of pain at rest.
Functional Status: Independent, but actively modifying behavior to avoid the specific threshold of exertion.
Social/Safety Concerns: Spouse is highly anxious that these episodes represent mini heart attacks.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 | HR: 76 | Temp: 36.5 C | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99 percent | Wt: 88 Kg
General: Comfortable at rest, conversing normally, no acute distress, no diaphoresis.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm. Heart sounds dual, no murmurs, no S3 or S4 gallops appreciated at rest. No elevated jugular venous pressure. Peripheral pulses are strong and symmetric. Respiratory: Lungs are completely clear to auscultation bilaterally. Abdomen: Soft, non-tender. DIAGNOSTIC TETRAD: Predictable exertional chest pain (by history), Relief with rest/nitrates within 10 minutes (by history), Transient ST depression (anticipated on formal exercise stress test).
Environmental Observations: House is situated on a steep incline, which acts as the daily exertional trigger.
Medication Reconciliation: Takes a daily statin and an antihypertensive. Compliant with medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Stable Angina Pectoris - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Unstable Angina / Acute Myocardial Infarction - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The pain strictly occurs only on exertion, has a highly predictable threshold, and resolves completely and rapidly with rest, with absolutely no pain occurring at rest.

Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The symptoms are strictly tied to walking (exertion) rather than meals or lying down, and radiate classically to the left arm.

Musculoskeletal Chest Pain - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Pain is described as heavy/squeezing rather than sharp, is not reproducible on chest wall palpation, and is not affected by torso movement or respiration.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with a textbook history of stable angina: predictable, exertional chest pain with a reproducible threshold, radiation to the arm, and rapid, complete relief with rest. The physical examination at rest is entirely normal, ruling out acute heart failure or ongoing ischemic damage. Urgent after-hours assessment was clinically justified to accurately differentiate this highly predictable pattern from unstable angina or an evolving myocardial infarction, thereby safely preventing an unnecessary emergency department admission overnight while initiating urgent fast-track outpatient investigations.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed sublingual Glyceryl Trinitrate (GTN) spray to be used immediately upon symptom onset or prophylactically before walking up the hill. Initiated Aspirin 100mg daily. Arranged an urgent outpatient 12-lead ECG and a referral for a treadmill exercise tolerance test or CT coronary angiogram.
Positive Behavioral Change: Instructed the patient to intentionally stop and rest before reaching the known pain threshold on the hill, and to use the new GTN spray prophylactically 5 minutes before the walk. By preemptively managing the oxygen demand and supply, the patient actively prevents the ischemic pain cycle from triggering. The reward is completing the walk pain-free, restoring confidence and safely maintaining mobility.
Home-Based Services: None.
DME/Safety: None.
Follow-up: Urgent General Practice review within 48-72 hours to review baseline ECG and expedite cardiology diagnostics.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the chest pain occurs while sitting quietly at rest, if the pain wakes the patient from sleep, or if the exertional pain does not completely resolve after 15 minutes and 3 doses of the GTN spray, an ambulance must be called immediately.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis relies on a classic clinical history; formal stress testing or imaging is required to visualize the coronary blockages; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: Stable blockages in the heart arteries cause predictable pain on exertion. Medications can control the symptoms, but formal testing determines if stents or bypass surgery are needed.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not push through the chest pain. Stop and rest immediately. Seek emergency care if the pain occurs at rest or does not go away after 15 minutes of resting.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The blood vessels supplying your heart have a stable narrowing. When you walk up the hill, your heart demands more blood, but the narrowed pipe can't deliver it fast enough, causing the heavy ache. Resting lowers the demand, and the pain stops. The new spray will help open the pipes temporarily.
Actionable Steps: Start taking the daily aspirin. Carry the GTN spray with you at all times. Stop walking the moment you feel the heavy sensation start.

